        "---\n",
        "title: \"Chapter 20. Evaluation of Language Models\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Metrics, Uncertainty, Calibration, and Responsible Measurement\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    code-fold: false\n",
        "  pdf:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "jupyter: python3\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Chapter 20. Evaluation of Language Models\n",
        "\n",
        "Evaluation is the mathematical discipline of asking whether a language system is doing what we want it to do.\n",
        "For a small classifier, evaluation may mean accuracy on a labeled test set.\n",
        "For a language model, evaluation may mean perplexity on held-out text.\n",
        "For a summarizer, evaluation may compare generated summaries with reference summaries.\n",
        "For a retrieval-augmented generation system, evaluation must include both retrieval quality and answer quality.\n",
        "For a deployed assistant, evaluation must also consider calibration, robustness, factuality, bias, latency, privacy, and human usefulness.\n",
        "\n",
        "The purpose of this chapter is not to collect many disconnected scores.\n",
        "The goal is to show that most evaluation methods have a common statistical structure:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{model} \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad \\text{predictions} \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad \\text{loss or score} \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad \\text{statistical estimate}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For MA Applied Mathematics and MS Statistics students, this chapter should feel familiar.\n",
        "Evaluation is estimation.\n",
        "A reported score is usually a sample average, a ratio of random counts, or a function of empirical distributions.\n",
        "Therefore, every evaluation score has assumptions, variance, failure modes, and a domain of validity.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Guiding principle\n",
        "A metric is not a property of a model alone.\n",
        "It is a property of a model, a data distribution, a task definition, an annotation procedure, and a scoring rule.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.1 Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. express NLP evaluation as estimation of expected loss or expected utility;\n",
        "2. compute accuracy, precision, recall, $F_1$, and confusion matrices from first principles;\n",
        "3. derive cross-entropy and perplexity for language models;\n",
        "4. explain calibration, Brier score, and expected calibration error;\n",
        "5. distinguish token-level, sequence-level, retrieval-level, and human-centered evaluation;\n",
        "6. implement overlap-based metrics such as ROUGE-style recall and BLEU-style precision;\n",
        "7. evaluate retrieval and RAG pipelines using Precision@$k$, Recall@$k$, MRR, and nDCG;\n",
        "8. quantify uncertainty in evaluation using standard errors and bootstrap confidence intervals;\n",
        "9. critique metric choice using mathematical reasoning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.2 The mathematical form of evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $(X,Y)$ be a random input-output pair drawn from an unknown population distribution $P$.\n",
        "A model $f_\\theta$ maps an input $x$ to either a prediction, a score vector, a probability distribution, or a generated sequence.\n",
        "A loss function compares the model output to the target:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(f_\\theta(X),Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The population risk is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R(\\theta) = E_P[L(f_\\theta(X),Y)].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $P$ is unknown, we estimate risk from a test set\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\text{test}} = \\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i=1}^n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The empirical risk is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n L(f_\\theta(x_i),y_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula is the mathematical backbone of evaluation.\n",
        "Different NLP tasks use different forms of $X$, $Y$, $f_\\theta$, and $L$.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Task | Input $X$ | Target $Y$ | Model output | Example score |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| Sentiment classification | document | class label | class probability | accuracy, $F_1$ |\n",
        "| Language modeling | prefix | next token | categorical distribution | cross-entropy, perplexity |\n",
        "| Summarization | document | reference summary | generated summary | ROUGE, human rating |\n",
        "| Retrieval | query | relevant documents | ranked list | Recall@$k$, MRR |\n",
        "| RAG answering | query plus corpus | answer | generated answer with evidence | faithfulness, answer correctness |\n",
        "\n",
        "The important statistical question is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R(\\theta) \\approx R(\\theta)?\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This approximation depends on sample size, test-set quality, distribution shift, and whether the metric faithfully measures the desired behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.3 Data splits and the danger of leakage\n",
        "\n",
        "A standard supervised workflow uses three data sets:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\text{train}}, \\qquad D_{\\text{validation}}, \\qquad D_{\\text{test}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The training set is used to fit parameters.\n",
        "The validation set is used to choose hyperparameters, thresholds, prompts, decoding strategies, retrieval settings, and checkpoints.\n",
        "The test set is used only once for final evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "If decisions are repeatedly made using the test set, then the test score becomes optimistically biased.\n",
        "This is a form of selection bias.\n",
        "For example, suppose we try many models $\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_m$ and report the best test score.\n",
        "Even if all models have the same population risk, the minimum empirical risk\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\min_{1 \\le j \\le m} \\widehat R(\\theta_j)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "will usually be smaller than the true risk of the selected model.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-warning}\n",
        "## Test-set leakage\n",
        "A test set is not a data bank for repeated design decisions.\n",
        "It is an estimator of future performance.\n",
        "Once it guides design choices, it starts functioning like a validation set.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.4 Classification metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider a classification task with classes $\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$.\n",
        "Let $y_i$ be the true class and $\\hat y_i$ be the predicted class.\n",
        "The accuracy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Accuracy}=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf 1\\{\\hat y_i=y_i\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy is simple and useful when classes are balanced and errors have similar cost.\n",
        "However, for imbalanced tasks, accuracy can be misleading.\n",
        "A classifier that always predicts the majority class may have high accuracy but low usefulness.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Binary confusion matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary classification, let the positive class be $1$ and the negative class be $0$.\n",
        "The confusion counts are:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "TP = \\sum_i \\mathbf 1\\{y_i=1,\\hat y_i=1\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "FP = \\sum_i \\mathbf 1\\{y_i=0,\\hat y_i=1\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "FN = \\sum_i \\mathbf 1\\{y_i=1,\\hat y_i=0\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "TN = \\sum_i \\mathbf 1\\{y_i=0,\\hat y_i=0\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures how reliable positive predictions are:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Precision}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall measures how many true positives are found:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Recall}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The $F_1$ score is the harmonic mean:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_1 = \\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{Precision}\\cdot \\operatorname{Recall}}{\\operatorname{Precision}+\\operatorname{Recall}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The harmonic mean is small if either precision or recall is small.\n",
        "Thus $F_1$ rewards balance.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Thresholds and decision rules\n",
        "\n",
        "Many classifiers output a score $s(x)$ or probability estimate $p_\\theta(y=1\\mid x)$.\n",
        "A threshold $\\tau$ produces a hard prediction:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat y_\\tau(x)=\\mathbf 1\\{p_\\theta(y=1\\mid x)\\ge \\tau\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing $\\tau$ changes $TP$, $FP$, $FN$, and $TN$.\n",
        "Therefore precision, recall, and $F_1$ are not properties of the probability model alone.\n",
        "They are properties of the probability model plus the threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Mathematical viewpoint\n",
        "A threshold is a decision rule.\n",
        "The same probabilistic model can produce many classifiers depending on how probabilities are converted into actions.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.5 Python example: classification metrics from scratch"
      ],
      "id": "d259916f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# A small binary text-classification evaluation example.\n",
        "y_true = np.array([1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0])\n",
        "p_score = np.array([0.92, 0.75, 0.62, 0.55, 0.48, 0.40, 0.36, 0.30, 0.22, 0.10])\n",
        "\n",
        "def binary_metrics(y_true, p_score, threshold=0.5):\n",
        "    y_pred = (p_score >= threshold).astype(int)\n",
        "    tp = int(np.sum((y_true == 1) & (y_pred == 1)))\n",
        "    fp = int(np.sum((y_true == 0) & (y_pred == 1)))\n",
        "    fn = int(np.sum((y_true == 1) & (y_pred == 0)))\n",
        "    tn = int(np.sum((y_true == 0) & (y_pred == 0)))\n",
        "    accuracy = (tp + tn) / len(y_true)\n",
        "    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp > 0 else 0.0\n",
        "    recall = tp / (tp + fn) if tp + fn > 0 else 0.0\n",
        "    f1 = 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall) if precision + recall > 0 else 0.0\n",
        "    return {\n",
        "        \"threshold\": threshold,\n",
        "        \"TP\": tp,\n",
        "        \"FP\": fp,\n",
        "        \"FN\": fn,\n",
        "        \"TN\": tn,\n",
        "        \"accuracy\": accuracy,\n",
        "        \"precision\": precision,\n",
        "        \"recall\": recall,\n",
        "        \"f1\": f1,\n",
        "    }\n",
        "\n",
        "for tau in [0.3, 0.5, 0.7]:\n",
        "    print(binary_metrics(y_true, p_score, tau))"
      ],
