        "## 20.7 Language-model evaluation: cross-entropy and perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "For a language model, the target is usually the next token.\n",
        "Given a sequence $w_{1:T}$, an autoregressive model assigns\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_{1:T}) = \\prod_{t=1}^T p_\\theta(w_t \\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The average negative log-likelihood per token is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat H = -\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(w_i \\mid \\text{context}_i),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $N$ is the number of evaluated target tokens.\n",
        "This quantity is also called empirical cross-entropy when the tokens are sampled from a data distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL} = \\exp(\\widehat H)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "when natural logarithms are used.\n",
        "If logarithms base $2$ are used, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}=2^{\\widehat H_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity can be interpreted as an effective average branching factor.\n",
        "A lower perplexity means the model assigns higher probability to the observed next tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Important limitations of perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity is useful for comparing models under the same tokenizer and evaluation distribution.\n",
        "However, it can be misleading across tokenizers.\n",
        "A model using shorter subword units may have a different number of tokens than a model using longer tokens.\n",
        "Per-token perplexity is therefore not automatically comparable across tokenization schemes.\n",
        "\n",
        "Also, low perplexity does not guarantee usefulness, factuality, safety, or reasoning quality.\n",
        "A model may predict common text well but fail on specialized mathematical or institutional questions."
      ],
      "id": "14372b61"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Token-level negative log-likelihood and perplexity.\n",
        "# Rows are time steps; columns are vocabulary items.\n",
        "logits = np.array([\n",
        "    [2.0, 1.0, 0.0],\n",
        "    [0.2, 1.8, 0.1],\n",
        "    [0.1, 0.4, 2.2],\n",
        "    [1.4, 0.8, 0.2],\n",
        "])\n",
        "\n",
        "targets = np.array([0, 1, 2, 1])\n",
        "\n",
        "def log_softmax(z):\n",
        "    z = z - np.max(z, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n",
        "    return z - np.log(np.sum(np.exp(z), axis=-1, keepdims=True))\n",
        "\n",
        "log_probs = log_softmax(logits)\n",
        "nll = -log_probs[np.arange(len(targets)), targets]\n",
        "mean_nll = np.mean(nll)\n",
        "perplexity = np.exp(mean_nll)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"token NLL values:\", np.round(nll, 4))\n",
        "print(\"mean NLL:\", round(mean_nll, 4))\n",
        "print(\"perplexity:\", round(perplexity, 4))"
      ],
