        "## 20.16 Robustness and distribution shift\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $P_{\\text{test}}$ be the distribution represented by the evaluation set and $P_{\\text{deploy}}$ be the distribution where the system is actually used.\n",
        "A model with low empirical risk on $P_{\\text{test}}$ may fail when\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_{\\text{deploy}} \\ne P_{\\text{test}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In NLP, distribution shift may come from:\n",
        "\n",
        "- a different domain;\n",
        "- a different institution;\n",
        "- new terminology;\n",
        "- multilingual or code-switched text;\n",
        "- different prompt styles;\n",
        "- adversarial inputs;\n",
        "- longer contexts;\n",
        "- updated policies or facts.\n",
        "\n",
        "Robust evaluation therefore should include subpopulation analysis.\n",
        "If the test set is partitioned into groups $G_1,\\ldots,G_m$, compute\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R_j = \\frac{1}{|G_j|}\\sum_{i\\in G_j} L(f_\\theta(x_i),y_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A single average can hide serious failures on smaller groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.17 Evaluation for mathematical NLP tasks\n",
        "\n",
        "For this book's audience, mathematical precision matters.\n",
        "A language model can be fluent but mathematically wrong.\n",
        "For math-heavy NLP tasks, evaluation should check:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **symbolic correctness:** Are formulas syntactically and mathematically valid?\n",
        "2. **logical correctness:** Does each conclusion follow from previous statements?\n",
        "3. **numerical correctness:** Are computations correct?\n",
        "4. **definition consistency:** Are terms used consistently?\n",
        "5. **assumption tracking:** Are required assumptions stated?\n",
        "6. **citation and source grounding:** For RAG, is every factual claim supported by evidence?\n",
        "\n",
        "A useful rubric for generated mathematical explanations may score each answer using a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "r = (r_{\\text{correct}}, r_{\\text{complete}}, r_{\\text{clear}}, r_{\\text{rigorous}}, r_{\\text{audience}}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The final evaluation may use a weighted score\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S = w^T r,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where weights $w$ should be chosen before evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.18 AI-assisted evaluation workflows\n",
        "\n",
        "Large language models can help with evaluation, but they should not be treated as infallible judges.\n",
        "A good AI-assisted workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. ask the AI judge to identify specific claims;\n",
        "2. ask it to compare each claim with the reference or retrieved evidence;\n",
        "3. require a structured score with reasons;\n",
        "4. audit a sample manually;\n",
        "5. measure agreement between AI judgments and human judgments.\n",
        "\n",
        "For mathematical material, an AI judge should be asked to separate:\n",
        "\n",
        "- algebraic correctness;\n",
        "- statistical interpretation;\n",
        "- notation consistency;\n",
        "- hidden assumptions;\n",
        "- relevance to the question.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important}\n",
        "## AI judge caution\n",
        "Using a language model to evaluate another language model creates a second modeling problem.\n",
        "The evaluator has its own biases, blind spots, and uncertainty.\n",
        "Treat AI-based evaluation as an instrument that must itself be validated.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Prompt pattern for AI-assisted evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "You are evaluating an NLP model answer for a graduate applied mathematics audience.\n",
        "Evaluate the answer using the following dimensions:\n",
        "1. mathematical correctness,\n",
        "2. statistical interpretation,\n",
        "3. notation consistency,\n",
        "4. completeness,\n",
        "5. clarity.\n",
        "For each dimension, give a score from 0 to 4 and cite the exact sentence or formula that supports your judgment.\n",
        "Do not reward fluent but unsupported claims.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This prompt is not a substitute for human evaluation.\n",
        "It is a way to make the evaluation process more structured.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 20.19 A compact evaluation checklist\n",
        "\n",
        "Before reporting an NLP evaluation score, answer the following questions.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. What is the target population distribution?\n",
        "2. What is the exact test set?\n",
